WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.572
An AI coding agent shouldn't just say a pull request looks good.

00:00:04.572 --> 00:00:09.143
It should help reproduce a bug, verify a change, and show evidence.

00:00:09.143 --> 00:00:15.052
Start with Node.js and Git. Run the offline try command. On our Windows

00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:20.961
test machine it verified seven architecture files, with zero Docker or AWS calls.

00:00:20.961 --> 00:00:26.319
Now use local Docker for real emulator API tests. This sample order handler

00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:31.678
sends two fulfillment events for one retried order. The reviewed fix sends one.

00:00:31.678 --> 00:00:36.737
What happens when DynamoDB saves an order but the S3 receipt upload

00:00:36.737 --> 00:00:41.796
times out? A fragile retry loses the event. Pending-state recovery restores it.

00:00:41.796 --> 00:00:47.407
You can compare two trusted Python handlers you own. Each runs in a separate

00:00:47.407 --> 00:00:53.018
isolated lab. The report checks the bug, the fix, source hashes, and cleanup.

00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:58.480
Before approving infrastructure changes, Architecture Review can flag a DynamoDB partition

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.941
key change. That's a source-level warning, not an AWS change set.

00:01:03.941 --> 00:01:08.612
Generate a local MCP configuration for your coding assistant. Then ask it

00:01:08.612 --> 00:01:13.283
to run a reviewed failure test and explain the evidence, not to guess.

00:01:13.283 --> 00:01:20.244
These reviewed scenarios passed GitHub CI with signed AWS Emulator version one point zero point four

00:01:20.244 --> 00:01:27.205
nine one. Local success does not prove AWS production parity or IAM enforcement. Your feedback helps.
